    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Teaching-Assistant-SPMA-2P16-Fall-D2-1_JR-1024168": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant SPMA 2P16 Fall D2-1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-17\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.   We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive !  Brock has been recognized as a Top Employer in Hamilton-Niagara for seven consecutive years. We have been ranked #3 as Canada’s Best Employers and top 10 as one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity. For 2025, Brock has been proudly recognized as one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture, ranked seventh by Forbes in partnership with Statista. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities.    Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: December 30, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) SPMA2P16 Governance and Policy in Sport Compensation  Teaching Assistant, Course Coordinator and Lab Demonstrator positions are paid according to the qualifications that the employee holds.    Employees who do NOT hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the undergraduate rate - $ 23.30   per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 202 6 )   Employees who hold a 4-year honours degree or a 3-year pass degree will be paid the graduate rate - $ 36.67   per hour.  (Rate effective September 1, 202 6 )   In cases where   additional   duties are required as part of a Teaching Assistant’s duties, these hours shall be compensated at the   appropriate Teaching   Assistant rate.    Duties  The duties and responsibilities of the Teaching Assistant shall be in accordance with Article 22 of the CUPE Local 4207, Unit 1, Collective Agreement. These are made up of scheduled contact time with students and non-classroom time (including preparation of seminar material, student consultation, marking or grading, examination proctoring, attending lectures, holding office hours, and other course administration as may be required. Qualifications  Applicants must be at least a fourth-year student student currently enrolled in a Bachelor of Sport Management, or equivalent; MA or higher will be given preference; · The successful applicant must have effective communication skills including the ability to explain subject material to students, be punctual in attending classes, and be able to meet appropriate deadlines; · Preferable to have taken the course with an 80% grade; · Proven track record of working effectively with competent and quick marking turnaround Applications  Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide seniority date within the application questionnaire.  Applications will be accepted until June 30 ,2026 Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval.  As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement:  Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time.   The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the   Office of People & Culture   website   -   https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/   Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/Temporary-Employment-Services--TES---Office-Assistant_JR-1020806": {
      "title": "Temporary Employment Services (TES), Office Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-01\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.   We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive !  Brock has been recognized as a Top Employer in Hamilton-Niagara for seven consecutive years. We have been ranked #3 as Canada’s Best Employers and top 10 as one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity. For 2025, Brock has been proudly recognized as one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture, ranked seventh by Forbes in partnership with Statista. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities.    Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: December 20, 2026 at 11:59 PM This posting is utilized to collect resumes for future vacancies. About the Role: The Temporary Employment Services (TES), Office Assistant will provide administrative support to various departments and faculties on short term work assignments to cover employee vacation, leaves of absence or extra assistance. A TES, Office Assistant may be scheduled up to 35 hours per week when required. The TES, Office Assistant will: Provide coverage and front-line administrative support on an as needed basis; Process forms, create employment contracts, cheque requisitions, travel claims, and purchase requisitions;  Schedule appointments and room bookings. Key Skills & Experience:  Demonstrated experience in an administrative environment, including front-line reception or customer service; Proficiency in computer applications including word processing, spreadsheets, databases, web applications, email,  Experience with bookkeeping, budget reconciliation, organizing events, database management including queries and reporting, mail merging, creating forms, organizing financial information and recordkeeping; Adaptability to undertake varying assignments of differing complexity or length, as well as be available on short notice; Ability to work independently or where supervision is minimal; Demonstrated communication skills with the ability to maintain confidentiality; Ability to learn new processes and procedures with accuracy and speed; Proven capability to adapt to changing environments. Salary  Job Grade (F), Salary Scale ($22.98-$31.87 per hour)*; Starting rate will be $22.98 per hour This position is included in the Ontario Secondary School Teachers Federation (OSSTF) Union and: Salary is therefore determined by the OSSTF Collective agreement. * New members shall be placed on the salary grid at the entry level. * It is Brock University’s policy to give first consideration to qualified internal Brock University OSSTF applicants. Applicants are required to upload a resume and cover letter as part of the application process.  We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Vice-Provost--Indigenous-Engagement_JR-1025313": {
      "title": "Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-05\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: September 29, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Senior Administrative Council (SAC) (Employee Group) Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement Brock University, Niagara Region, Ontario Brock University is seeking a respected, relationship-centered Indigenous leader to serve as its next Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement. This is a unique opportunity to help shape the next chapter of Indigenous engagement, reconciliation, and institutional transformation at Brock University. Building on a strong foundation of community partnerships, Indigenous programming, and institutional commitment, the successful candidate will play a central role in implementing Brock's Indigenous Strategic Plan and fostering meaningful, sustainable change throughout the University. The University Brock University is a comprehensive teaching and research institution with rich academic programs and world-class scholarship. Located on the traditional lands of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement, with its main campus located in the historic Niagara region, Brock offers all the benefits of a young and modern university in a safe, community-minded region with beautiful natural surroundings. With more than 18,000 students in seven diverse Faculties, 600 faculty members, and over 124,000 accomplished alumni, Brock has earned a reputation as a one of Canada’s top post-secondary institutions. The university continues be an important regional presence and with a new campus in Burlington, further growing connections to the community through this new, dynamic space. Brock was recently   named one of Canada's best employers for Company Culture by Forbes   as well as   ranked top 10 as one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2024 ,   through an independent survey of more than 40,000 Canadian employees working for companies with more than 500 employees. Brock is also home to fourteen 3M Teaching Fellowship Award Winners, Canada’s most prestigious recognition of excellence in educational leadership and teaching at the post-secondary level, and eight Canada Research Chairs. The Role Reporting to the Provost and Vice-President, Academic, the Vice-Provost, Indigenous Engagement serves as the University's senior Indigenous academic leader and a key member of Brock's academic leadership team. The VPIE provides strategic advice and leadership across the institution to advance Indigenous priorities and strengthen relationships with Indigenous communities, students, faculty, staff, Elders, Knowledge Holders, and partners. Key Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership and Institutional Transformation   – Provide leadership for the implementation of Brock's Indigenous Strategic Plan and related institutional priorities. Support the development of measurable goals, accountability frameworks, and institution-wide initiatives that advance reconciliation, decolonization, and Indigenous engagement across academic and administrative portfolios. Serve as an advisor to the institution, working closely with senior leaders, faculty, staff, and students to build capacity for Indigenous engagement and cultural understanding. Community Engagement and Partnerships   – Serve as Brock's primary senior contact for Indigenous community engagement. Build and maintain respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, leadership organizations, governments, educational institutions, and community partners. Support meaningful engagement with Indigenous advisory and governance bodies while helping strengthen alignment between community priorities and institutional processes. Student Success and Well-being   – Champion Indigenous student success by supporting culturally grounded programs, services, and initiatives that foster belonging, achievement, leadership development, community connection, and holistic well-being throughout the student experience. Academic Leadership and Indigenization   – Provide leadership and guidance regarding Indigenous governance structures, institutional policies, and community-informed decision-making. Collaborate with Deans, faculty leaders, researchers, and academic units to advance Indigenous curriculum development, support Indigenous research and scholarship, build relationships with Indigenous faculty and staff, strengthen community-engaged research, and promote respectful and reciprocal approaches to knowledge creation and dissemination. The Ideal Candidate As the ideal candidate, you are an accomplished Indigenous leader in the higher education sector who is recognized for your ability to build trust, strengthen relationships, and guide organizations through meaningful change. You are a courageous leader, bringing a demonstrated commitment to Indigenous advancement and possess the credibility, humility, and strategic leadership required to work effectively across Indigenous communities and complex institutional environments.   You understand that meaningful progress is rooted in both relationships and action. You are a skilled listener, collaborator, and advocate who can bring diverse groups together around a shared vision while ensuring priorities are translated into measurable outcomes. Whether your experience has been developed primarily within higher education, Indigenous organizations, public institutions, community leadership, or a combination of these environments, you bring a demonstrated record of advancing Indigenous priorities, leading organizational change, and fostering strong partnerships. Qualifications and Demonstrated Success: Indigenous identity and lived experience, preferably connected to local communities, consistent with the University's intention to fill this role as a designated appointment for an Indigenous candidate. An earned doctorate, terminal degree, or an equivalent combination of education, community leadership, executive experience, and demonstrated accomplishments. Progressively senior leadership experience within post-secondary education, Indigenous organizations, government, public sector organizations, or similarly complex environments. Demonstrated success building meaningful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, organizations, Elders, Knowledge Holders, and partners. Experience leading strategic initiatives, organizational change, and collaborative planning processes. Understanding of Indigenous education, Indigenous student success, Indigenous scholarship, and community-engaged approaches to teaching, learning, and research. Knowledge of Indigenous governance, policy environments, and contemporary issues affecting First Nations, Métis, and Inuit peoples. Experience working collaboratively within complex organizations and advising senior leaders, governing bodies, or community decision-makers. Skills and Attributes: Exceptional relationship-building and consensus-building skills and a collaborative, authentic, and community-centered leadership style. Strategic thinking and action-oriented, with strong implementation abilities. Excellent communication, facilitation, and engagement skills. Courage as a leader with a commitment to accountability, transparency, and shared responsibility. Humility, integrity, resilience, and cultural grounding. Understanding of distinctions-based approaches and the diverse realities of First Nations, Métis, Inuit, and urban, global and reconnecting Indigenous communities. Role Type: Current Vacancy The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications on September 30, 2026. KBRS does not use artificial intelligence in any part of candidate and application screening. Brock University recognizes that it is located on the traditional lands of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement. Today, Brock is the gathering place of many First Nations, Métis and Inuit — many of whom continue to live and work on its campus. Brock University is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive environment and encourages applications from all qualified individuals, including but not limited to women, Indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities, racialized persons, and persons of any gender identity and sexual orientation. Support and accommodations will be provided to applicants with disabilities, upon request. Applicants in need of an accommodation in order to participate as a candidate in the recruitment process can contact   accommodate@kbrs.ca   or identify their needs to the recruitment professional named in the job description. KBRS does not use artificial intelligence to screen candidate applications. Candidates interested in this opportunity should contact Abbey MacLeod at  amacleod@kbrs.ca , Kyle Steele at   ksteele@kbrs.ca , or Dr. Jennie Massey at  jmassey@kbrs.ca , or submit a full application package by clicking \"Apply Now\" by visiting the following link: https://www.kbrs.ca/Career/17744448320300000008kiz We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Budget-Officer_JR-1025420": {
      "title": "Budget Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 23, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role:    The Budget officer provides expert financial support for the Faculty of Education, ensuring effective budget development, monitoring, and compliance with institutional policies. The Budget Officer combines analytical and operational responsibilities, including forecasting, variance analysis, transaction oversight, and teaching load reconciliation. They act as the faculty’s primary financial resource, supporting academic leadership with data-driven insights and ensuring efficient financial operations.    The Budget Officer will:     Prepare financial forecasts and conduct variance analysis on actual-to-budget comparisons;  Conduct financial analyses, projections and reporting on course enrolment, including low enrolment analysis;  Investigate significant variances and provide actionable recommendations to improve performance;  Maintain accurate and timely reporting for faculty leadership, including trimester projections and consolidated financial summaries;  Perform financial analysis for faculty projects, and program accounts;  Review and approve faculty financial transactions, ensuring adherence to university policies and funding requirements;  Process journal entries, reconcile accounts and monitor deficit/ended accounts  Provide guidance to faculty and staff on financial policies, expense categories, and best practices;  Coordinate and prepare the faculty’s annual budget submission, working closely with academic units and administrative staff;  Develop supporting schedules and templates for budget processes, ensuring accuracy and compliance with university guidelines;  Provide financial input for Faculty planning initiatives and advise on budget impacts of operational decisions;  Support special projects and initiatives, while providing finance and accounting expertise; and  Identify opportunities for process improvement and contribute to operational efficiency.    Key Skills and Experience:    An undergraduate degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or related discipline or a combination of education and experience;  Strong knowledge of financial systems, reporting tools, and Microsoft Excel;   Skilled in budgeting, forecasting, and variance analysis;    Promotes and embodies inclusivity and respect within the workplace and the broader community;   Strong communication and interpersonal skills;    Works effectively with faculty leadership, staff, and central services;     Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and organizational skills;     Ability to interpret and apply policies and provide guidance to stakeholders;    Ensures accuracy in financial reporting and compliance;     Provides practical solutions to financial and operational challenges; and    Responds effectively to changing priorities and deadlines.      Salary and Total Rewards:    Job Grade [K], Salary Scale ($61,286.00 - $99,384.00  Target Hiring Range: $61,286.00 - $65,000.00  This is a 12 Month contract position that includes:    Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage.  Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan.  Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.  Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program.  *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Graduate-Physics-Preparation-Instructor-PHYS-5N02-Winter-D3_JR-1025429": {
      "title": "Graduate Physics Preparation Instructor PHYS 5N02 Winter D3",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nPart time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 15, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the CUPE4207-1 (Employee Group) Compensation Effective September 1, 2026 - $7,887.59 per half course (195 hours nominally) Effective September 1, 2026 - $15,775.20 per full course (390 hours nominally) Please note: Instructors who are employed in a 2-hour/week lecture, or the equivalent of a 2-hour/week lecture, are responsible for the first hour of seminar/lab in each course. Duties The nominal workload for this position is approximately 195 hours per semester.   Reporting to the Physics Graduate Program Director, the Graduate Physics Preparation Instructor will develop and deliver a preparatory course for new graduate students in Materials Physics (MSMP) whose first language is not English. The objective of this course is to enable development of English language skills in a science, especially physics, context, prior to proceeding to the MSMP courses. The course should cover the following topics: (i) critical thinking and synthesizing arguments and ideas, (ii) researching, referencing and academic integrity, (iii) academic presentations and discussions, (iv) development of specialized vocabulary, and (v)  academic note taking.   The instructor is responsible for the preparation of weekly lesson plans, tests, and assignments, in accordance to the requirements of the MSMP curriculum. The instructor is also responsible for the marking and evaluation of student work. Additionally, the job will involve coordinating the orientation sessions for the MSMP students with ITS, Library, and other relevant Brock services and facilities, including laboratory safety training.  Duties and responsibilities will be in accordance with Article 22.01 of the Collective Agreement. These include scheduled contact time with students and non-classroom time (preparation of lectures, student consultation, marking and grading and course administration, including grade appeals and cases of academic dishonesty).  Qualifications In most circumstances, the minimum qualifications shall be: (1) Bachelor degree in a relevant field; (2) a background in science and scientific writing shall be taken into consideration; (3) post-secondary teaching experience, especially in  ESL/EFL, is very desirable; (4) demonstrated interpersonal qualities  that support international students’ course-related needs.  The applicant must demonstrate successful experience in teaching at a university level.    Applications Please include a cover letter and a current resume. Members with seniority can provide their seniority date in the application questionnaire.   For further information     Please contact:   Ivana Komljenovic Metcalf  Physics   ikmetcalf@brocku.ca     Please Note     If applicable, applicants are responsible for submitting their seniority date with their application. Applicants that don't include a seniority date will be assumed not to have one.    Positions listed on this posting are subject to course enrolments and budgetary approval. As per Article 22.04 of the Collective Agreement: Members shall not accept any appointment which, taken together with all other appointments at the University, would cause the member to exceed the maximum number of regular hours (i.e., not overtime hours) allowable in a work week under the Employment Standards Act, as amended from time to time. Applicants that are in excess of the above noted workload limits will not have their applications considered unless they have received prior written approval from the appropriate Dean and the Human Resources Department. The Collective Agreement governs part-time Instructor, Teaching Assistant, Lab Demonstrator, Course Coordinator and Marker-Grader positions at Brock University. The Agreement can be accessed on the Human Resources Web Site - https://brocku.ca/human-resources/collective-agreements/ Additional Comments  Course descriptions can be found on the Undergraduate course calendar website:  http://www.brocku.ca/webcal/   Academic terms generally run from: Fall – September to December Winter – January to April Spring – May to July Summer – July to August *Actual dates can be found on the Timetables available on the Office of the Registrar web page: https://brocku.ca/registrar/toolkit/registration/ We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Associate-Director--Experiential-Education_JR-1025418": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Experiential Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 18, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role:  Reporting to the Director, Co-op, Career, & Experiential Education, the Associate Director, Experiential Education works in partnership to translate CCEE's strategic direction into effective Experiential Education (EE) operations, initiatives and partnerships, while bringing forward evidence, emerging opportunities and recommendations to inform portfolio-level planning and decision-making. The incumbent leads a complex, multidisciplinary team working across diverse Faculty and academic contexts. The incumbent provides direction that supports consistency and alignment across the EE portfolio while recognizing the distinct needs, relationships, priorities, and operating contexts of individual Faculties. The incumbent oversees experiential education policy, programming, operations and continuous improvement, and represents the University on matters pertaining to experiential education.     The Associate Director, Experiential Education provides leadership and direction to the EE team, establishing shared priorities, decision-making approaches and ways of working that support effective collaboration, accountability and service across the University. The incumbent is also responsible for creating and nurturing relationships with the broader team of Co-op, Career and Experiential Education (CCEE) and other critical internal and external partners to the University, including community organizations and other academic institutions. They promote knowledge mobilization and management of day-to-day operations alongside supporting CCEE’s leadership in co-operative, career and experiential learning.    The Associate Director, Experiential Education will:   Develop and uphold strategy to engage and retain local, national and international organizations for the purpose of developing curricular-based experiential learning partnerships;  Provide leadership and guidance to EE staff in navigating complex Faculty, departmental and institutional relationships;  Ensure that Faculty relationships and individual portfolio approaches remain connected to broader CCEE and University priorities;  Work with community and institutional partners to identify appropriate approaches to evaluating partnerships and EE initiatives, and use findings to strengthen relationships, programs and outcomes;  Oversee and ensure collaboration with cross-functional multi-disciplinary experiential education staff and Faculty to maintain consistent practice, data reporting and tracking;    Establish shared approaches, principles and decision-making frameworks that promote consistency across EE while allowing appropriate flexibility;  Lead the identification, development, implementation and evaluation of initiatives that improve program quality, partnerships, student and partner satisfaction, staff capability and performance;    Ensure equity, diversity and inclusion best practices are embedded at the foundational level of all experiential education services;  Oversee and ensure the maintenance and engagement with a database of EE opportunities at local, national, and international levels;  Develop and oversee appropriate approaches to evaluate EE programs, services, partnerships and initiatives;  Analyze and interpret quantitative and qualitative evidence to assess program effectiveness, identify trends and opportunities for improvement, and inform strategic decision-making;  Manage the Experiential Education budget and contribute to the CCEE annual budget planning process, aligning resources with established strategic priorities;  Translate CCEE and University strategic priorities into operational plans, initiatives and priorities for Experiential Education;  Oversee the administration and stewardship of external funding received to support experiential education initiatives;  Ensure alignment of EE work, resources and processes across Faculties and departments while recognizing appropriate differences in Faculty context and program needs.    Key Skills and Experience:  Experience in the field of experiential education, adult education with program and policy development;  Experience in a leadership and/or supervisory role, including strategic planning functions;  Demonstrated experience leading multidisciplinary teams in complex organizational environments with multiple partners and competing priorities;  Strong understanding of experiential education theories and the strategic role career and experiential education has in a university environment;  Demonstrated effectiveness in adult education, teaching and facilitation;    Strong interpersonal and organizational skills, with the ability to communicate with executive level internal and external partners;  Demonstrated knowledge and experience in program evaluation, assessment and continuous improvement, with the ability to use evidence and feedback to inform program and service development;  Competency with budgeting, data, strategic, and financial planning;  Promotes and embodies inclusivity and respect within the workplace and the broader community;  Demonstrated ability to build and maintain collaborative relationships with faculty, partners, and local, provincial, national, and international partners while serving as a liaison between the University and external communities;  Strong analytical, critical thinking, and problem-solving skills;  Proven ability to manage multiple projects, deadlines, and priorities independently while adapting to change, ambiguity, and evolving organizational needs;  Ability to leverage resources effectively, conduct research, disseminate knowledge, and support continuous learning and improvement;  Strong organizational, time management, prioritization, teamwork, and integrity-focused work practices.  Salary and Total Rewards: Job Grade O, Salary Scale ($90,294- 144,470); Target Hiring Range: $90,294-$104,000* This is a permanent position that includes:  Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage.  Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan.  Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays.  Flexible Work Arrangements: Hybrid work schedule available.  Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program.  *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track--Early-Childhood-Education-and-Care--Equitable--Inclusive--and-Sustainable-Practices_JR-1025406": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Early Childhood Education and Care: Equitable, Inclusive, and Sustainable Practices",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nThis position is part of the BUFA (Employee Group) Brock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: Note to all candidates: This posting will close at 12:01 am on the date listed . November 13, 2026 About Brock University The Brock University experience is second to none in Canada. Located in the historic Niagara region, Brock offers all the benefits of a young and modern university in a safe, community-minded city, with beautiful natural surroundings. With over 18,000 students and more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in seven diverse Faculties, Brock excels at providing exceptional experiential learning opportunities and highly rated student and campus life experiences. Our Geography Brock University’s main campus is situated atop the Niagara Escarpment, within a UNESCO World Biosphere Reserve, overlooking the city of St. Catharines, in the heart of Niagara wine country. The Niagara region is dotted with landmarks that recognize our nation’s history and features breathtaking natural beauty and world-famous attractions. St. Catharines is home to vibrant arts and entertainment venues, and is a short drive from Toronto, Niagara Falls, and Buffalo, New York. With one of the warmest climates in Canada, clean, safe communities, and surprisingly affordable real estate, Niagara is an exceptional location to call home. What we Offer Brock University offers competitive salary and benefits and ample support for research and sabbaticals. Research resources include; conference support, start-up funding, subscriptions to major databases and access to various research funding vehicles. For candidates considering relocation, moving expenses will be administered according to the Faculty Association Collective Agreement. About the Faculty The Faculty of Education at Brock University, with a proud history dating back to 1965, is home to nearly 33,000 alumni and currently serves a vibrant community of undergraduate and graduate students across its St. Catharines and Burlington campuses. Renowned for its excellence in teaching, research, and community engagement, the faculty offers a range of innovative programs. Our mission is to educate exceptional educators by fostering academic excellence, promoting experiential learning, and engaging meaningfully with Indigenous and community partners. Our faculty members are committed to scholarship, teaching, and service that foster anti-racist, decolonial, and sustainable communities—communities that affirm human and nonhuman onto-epistemological diversity and advance equity. In alignment with Brock University’s strategic plan (2025-2030), this commitment is reflected in efforts to promote inclusion, reconciliation, accessibility, and decolonization across teaching, research, and community engagement ( https://brocku.ca/strategic-plan/ ). About the Bachelor of Early Childhood Education Program The Bachelor of Early Childhood Education (B.ECE) Honours program is a college-transfer undergraduate degree designed to support the continued growth and professionalization of early learning and care professionals committed to reflective, ethical, and evidence-informed practice. This online asynchronous program prepares educators to critically examine and reconceptualize conventional understandings of early learning and development, equipping them to engage thoughtfully with diverse perspectives, challenge inequitable practices, and contribute to more just and sustainable early childhood education and care (ECEC) communities. The program is unique and heralded both within the university and nationally as a quality program that positions students with the skills and competencies needed to meet the escalating labour market demands within the Canada-Wide Early Learning and Child Care (CWELCC) agreement system. About the Position The Department of Educational Studies invites applicants for a Tenure Track Appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning January 1st, 2027. Area of Expertise Within an ECEC context, equitable, inclusive, and sustainable practices encompass pedagogical approaches, policies, and early learning environments that promote equity, inclusion, social responsibility, cultural integrity, and environmental stewardship for children, families, educators, and communities. These practices uphold children's rights; value diverse identities, perspectives, and ways of knowing; address systemic inequities; and foster belonging, participation, and collective well-being. Examples of relevant areas of study may include: equity, social justice, and inclusion; environmental and sociocultural sustainability; broader structural, political, and community impact; ethics, futures, and intergenerational responsibility; anti-biased education and respect; agency, participation, and citizenship. Responsibilities of this position include: Teaching in the B.ECE online asynchronous program including, but not limited to, one or more of the following areas: Indigenous Culture & ECE; Ethics, Policy & Care in ECE; ECE for Sustainability. Commitment to supporting the quality of the B.ECE program and Department of Educational Studies, including commitment to Indigenous practices and decolonial education; equity and inclusion; justice, ethics, and respect. Graduate and undergraduate student mentorship and supervision. Establishing and maintaining an innovative and productive research program in ECEC. Service contributions to the department, the university, and ECEC community. Qualifications The Department of Educational Studies seeks candidates who are committed to equitable, inclusive, and sustainable practices in ECEC through research, teaching, and service. Required: PhD (or ABD) or equivalent in Early Childhood Education or closely related field (e.g., Early Childhood Studies, Curriculum and Pedagogy in the Early Years, Educational Studies with a focus on Early Childhood) or equivalent Indigenous knowledges1, specializing in Early Childhood Education. Demonstrated commitment to the field of ECEC, advancing equitable, inclusive, and sustainable approaches, including, but not limited to, anti-oppression, decolonial, reconciliation, anti-racist, anti-homophobic, anti-transphobic, anti-xenophobic, gender equity, and socioeconomic justice through course design, research endeavours, and teaching approaches. Successful teaching experience in ECEC at the post-secondary level. Professional experience in ECEC settings, programs, or services (e.g., child care, pre-school, pre- kindergarten or kindergarten2, community programs, extended care programs for school-aged children, child and family services). Familiarity with the ECEC field's ongoing challenges and emerging directions in relation to research, theory, practice, and/or policy. Preferred: Proficiency in inclusive, accessible, and effective course design and asynchronous digital pedagogy, with awareness of the implications of artificial intelligence for teaching and learning. Established connections with the local, national, and/or international ECEC community at the scholarly, governmental, and/or practice levels. An ECE Diploma, recognized professional ECE Certification or registration, or a comparable ECE- specific credential from another jurisdiction. 1. In determining a Ph.D. equivalency for Indigenous knowledge, consideration shall be given, but not limited to, the level of knowledge of the language and/or traditional customs including protocols, spirituality, traditions, practices, ceremonies, histories, and teachings of a particular group of Indigenous people or peoples. This knowledge may have been acquired through lived experience; active and lengthy participation in Indigenous forms of self-determination and governance, cultural structures, and processes; and a careful study and reflection of their philosophical underpinnings. In many cases, acquiring this knowledge will have involved studying with an Indigenous Elder or Traditional Knowledge Carrier/Keeper.   2. For experience obtained within kindergarten settings, the applicant must have practiced in an early childhood educator role.   What to Include in the Application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter (~3 pages) demonstrating the candidate’s alignment with the posting. Teaching Dossier that includes: a teaching statement (~2 pages) that articulates the candidate's philosophy, experience, and pedagogical approaches to preparing ethical ECEC professionals. The statement should demonstrate commitment to relational and culturally sustaining pedagogy, diverse Indigenous and non-dominant knowledge systems, and transformative practices that promote Indigenous knowledges, sovereignty and relationships and Equity, Diversity, Inclusion, and Belonging; and evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., sample course outlines and assessments, mentoring experiences, student and/or peer evaluations). Note. We recognize that teaching evaluations are not necessarily indicative of one’s teaching strengths and capacity. Therefore, candidates need not submit evaluations but ought to demonstrate teaching impact and effectiveness in academic and/or non-traditional educational contexts. Research Statement (~2 pages) describing the candidate’s program of research, major contributions, and future research plans. Three Referees : Names and contact information for three academic referees (who will only be contacted for short-listed candidates). All documents must be submitted in electronic format (a single well-organized PDF document is preferred) via the online application system (file maximum of 5MB per attachment upload). The closing date for applications is November 13, 2026 at 12:01am. The final full day to apply is November 12, 2026. Inquiries should be directed to Wanda Burger, Executive Assistant, foedean@brocku.ca. The target hiring range for this position is $ 100,000 to $118,000. The final salary will be determined based on relevant experience, internal equity and budget considerations. This ad is for an existing position in the Brock University Faculty Association (BUFA) bargaining unit. This position is subject to final budgetary approval. Our Commitment Brock University is actively committed to diversity and the principles of employment equity and invites applications from all qualified candidates. Women, Indigenous (First Nations, Métis, Inuit) peoples, Black people, people with disabilities, members of visible minorities/racialized groups, and Two-Spirit, lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, queer, intersex, and additional sexually and gender diverse (2SLGBTQI+) persons are encouraged to apply and to voluntarily self-identify as a member of a designated group as part of their application. Candidates who wish to be considered as a member of one or more designated groups should fill out the Self-Identification Form and include the completed form with their application. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. We will accommodate the needs of the applicants and the Ontario Human Rights Code and the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA) throughout all stages of the selection process, as outlined in the Employment Accommodation Policy https://brocku.ca/policies/wp-content/uploads/sites/94/Employment-Accommodation-Policy.pdf . Please advise: talent@brocku.ca   to ensure your accessibility needs are accommodated through this process. Information received relating to accommodation measures will be addressed confidentially. We appreciate all applications received; however, only candidates selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca"
    },
